const lessons=[
 ['The speaking test, explained','Test essentials','Learn what happens in Parts 1, 2 and 3.','The speaking test has three parts. Part 1 covers familiar questions. In Part 2 you prepare for one minute, then speak for up to two minutes. Part 3 develops more abstract ideas related to the cue card. Practise moving from a personal answer to a wider discussion.','Try answering “What do you enjoy about where you live?” Then explain how cities could become better places to live.'],
 ['Turn a short answer into a story','Answer expansion','Answer → reason → example → reflection.','Start with a direct answer. Explain why you feel that way. Add a real example, then finish with a short reflection. You do not need to force all four steps into every Part 1 answer. Keep each detail relevant.','Instead of “I like reading”, try “I enjoy reading because it helps me unwind. Last week I finished a mystery novel on my commute.”'],
 ['Find your natural flow','Fluency','Practise continuity without rushing.','Speak for 45 seconds about a familiar activity. When you need a moment, pause naturally. Avoid restarting an entire sentence for a small mistake. Review one recording and notice where an unfinished thought made your meaning unclear.','Describe your morning routine. Repeat the task, keeping your main ideas but reducing unnecessary restarts.'],
 ['Grammar you can actually use','Spoken grammar','Build accurate sentences, one pattern at a time.','Use the present perfect continuous for something that began in the past and is still happening. “I have been living here for five years.” Use “for” with a duration and “since” with a starting point. A natural contraction is “I’ve been living here…”','Say how long you have been studying English. Then say when you started.'],
 ['Vocabulary that sounds like you','Natural vocabulary','Choose accurate expressions over difficult words.','Simple language can be excellent language. Replace vague words when a more precise expression fits your meaning. “A lively neighbourhood” tells us more than “a very good place”. Do not add idioms just to sound advanced.','Describe your hometown using “lively”, “convenient” or “peaceful”, only if the word matches your experience.'],
 ['Paraphrase without losing meaning','Collocations','Express the same idea in more than one way.','Paraphrasing is flexible expression, not word-for-word substitution. “I like spending time outdoors” can become “I enjoy being outside”. Some words naturally go together, such as “make a decision” and “gain experience”.','Give two natural ways to say “I like learning new things”.'],
 ['Make your meaning easy to follow','Coherence','Connect ideas with a clear purpose.','Use connections that match your ideas: “because” for a reason, “for example” for an illustration, and “although” for a contrast. A long list of linking phrases does not automatically make an answer coherent.','Explain a preference, give a reason, then mention one drawback.'],
 ['Build a two-minute cue-card answer','Part 2 strategy','Use your preparation minute well.','Write a few keywords for each cue point, not a script. Build your answer around a specific event or experience. Cover the important points, then develop why the experience mattered to you.','Choose a memorable journey. Note where, when, who and why, then record an answer.'],
 ['Discuss ideas with confidence','Part 3 strategy','Consider causes, effects and alternative views.','State a view, explain your reasoning and support it with an example. If the issue has more than one side, acknowledge a reasonable alternative. Avoid presenting every personal opinion as an established fact.','Should cities invest more in public transport? Give one advantage and one challenge.'],
 ['Be understood clearly','Pronunciation','Focus on intelligibility, stress and rhythm.','You do not need to imitate a particular accent. Group words into meaningful phrases and stress important words. Record a short sentence and listen for sounds or word boundaries that make your message hard to understand.','Say “One of the main reasons is convenience.” Stress “main reasons” and “convenience”.'],
 ['Finish the thought','Sentence structure','Notice fragments and unfinished ideas.','A spoken answer can contain short phrases naturally. A problem occurs when the listener cannot understand your intended meaning. After recording, find any sentence where you started an explanation but never completed it.','Complete this idea in your own words: “The main thing I would change about my city is…”'],
 ['Review, retry, remember','Deliberate practice','Turn feedback into a useful habit.','Choose one correction. Understand why it matters, then answer the same question again without reading a model answer. Compare the attempts. Keep corrections available even after you mark them reviewed.','Practise one saved grammar correction in three new sentences.'],
 ['Part 1: personal and specific','Part 1 strategy','Give a clear answer with a useful detail.','Respond directly to the question. Add a brief reason or a real detail. Avoid memorised introductions and long speeches for a simple personal question.','Answer “Do you enjoy cooking?” in two or three natural sentences.'],
 ['Pause with purpose','Hesitation','Give yourself thinking space.','A brief pause is natural. Excessive filler and repeated restarts can make an answer harder to follow. Try pausing at the end of a thought rather than halfway through a phrase.','Speak for 30 seconds about your weekend and listen for repeated fillers.'],
 ['Stress and intonation','Speech rhythm','Use your voice to support your meaning.','Content words often carry stress. Intonation can show whether you have finished a thought, are asking a question, or are expressing uncertainty. Listen to your recording and notice whether your phrasing helps the listener.','Read the same sentence once as a statement and once as a surprised question.'],
 ['Speak with more flexibility','Advanced development','Develop a balanced, nuanced response.','Qualify broad claims when appropriate: “in some cases”, “often” and “it depends on…” can make your meaning more accurate. Use examples to explain nuance, rather than adding complexity for its own sake.','Discuss whether working from home suits everyone. Consider two different types of worker.']
];
